        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What humidity level causes tools to rust?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Corrosion becomes practically significant above about 60 percent relative humidity and proceeds quickly above 70. Below 50 percent it is slow enough to ignore on coated tools. Temperature swings matter as much as the average, because condensation forms whenever steel is colder than the air around it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is WD-40 good for preventing rust on tools?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is good at displacing water after a wet job and poor as long-term protection, because the film it leaves is thin and evaporates within weeks. Use it to dry out a wet mechanism, then follow with light machine oil, paste wax or a dedicated corrosion inhibitor for actual storage."
          }
