    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A socket set is the cheapest thing in a workshop to protect and the most commonly neglected. Rust does not need a leak or a spill; it needs humid air and time, both of which an unheated garage supplies for free. Fixing the air is more effective than any coating you can put on the steel.
        </p>

        <div id="why-tools-rust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Tools Actually Rust
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rust is iron combining with oxygen in the presence of water. The water rarely arrives as a spill — it arrives as humidity condensing out of the air onto cold steel, which happens every time the temperature swings.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why an unheated garage is such a reliable rust factory. Cold nights chill the tools; a warmer, damper morning brings air that condenses on them. The steel spends part of every day with a microscopic film of water on it, and over a season that adds up to exactly the conditions rust needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The threshold worth knowing is around <strong>60 percent relative humidity</strong>. Below that, corrosion is slow enough to be practically irrelevant on a coated tool. Above it, and particularly above 70 percent, rust proceeds at a pace you will see within months.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things accelerate it sharply. Salt — from coastal air, winter road spray, or sweaty hands — dramatically increases the conductivity of that water film and therefore the corrosion rate. And existing rust is itself porous and hygroscopic, so it holds moisture against the metal and spreads faster than it started.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Thing Most People Get Wrong</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fix the air, not the steel.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A dehumidifier holding a garage under 60 percent protects every tool in it, permanently, including the ones you forget to oil. Coatings are worth applying, but they are the second line of defence, not the first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="humidity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Controlling the Air
          </h2>

          <p>
            Everything else in this guide is secondary to keeping the humidity down in the space where the tools live.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Dehumidifier</h3>

          <p>
            The most effective single measure for a garage, basement or workshop. Set it to hold 50 to 55 percent and it protects the whole room without you touching a tool. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">dehumidifier guide</a> covers sizing for unheated spaces, which is the usual mistake — a unit rated for a living room struggles in a cold garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Desiccant in Closed Boxes</h3>

          <p>
            Inside a closed toolbox or a drawer, silica gel packs create a small dry microclimate cheaply. Buy the rechargeable indicating type, which changes colour when saturated and can be dried in an oven and reused indefinitely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Insulation and Air Movement</h3>

          <p>
            Condensation forms when steel is colder than the air around it, so anything that slows temperature swings helps. Keeping tool storage off an outside wall and off a concrete floor — both of which run cold and wick moisture — makes a measurable difference for no running cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat, Gently</h3>

          <p>
            A low-wattage tube heater or a light bulb in an enclosed cabinet keeps the contents a few degrees above ambient, which is enough to stop condensation forming on them. It costs pennies and is the classic solution in gun safes and tool chests for exactly this reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coatings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Coatings That Actually Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            With the air handled, a barrier on the steel covers the rest. Several options, with real differences.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lasts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">VCI paper or capsules</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Closed drawers and boxes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vapour protects everything in the space</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light machine oil</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-6 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">All bare steel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cheap, easy, needs reapplying</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Paste wax</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-12 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tool surfaces and table tops</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dry to the touch, does not attract dust</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Corrosion-inhibiting spray</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-12 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Long-term storage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leaves a film that must be cleaned off before use</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">WD-40 type products</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weeks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Displacing water after a wet job</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A water displacer, not a long-term protectant</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The last row is worth dwelling on because it is the most common misunderstanding in this whole subject. Water-displacing sprays are excellent at what their name says — driving moisture out of a wet mechanism — but the film they leave is thin and evaporates. Using one as your rust prevention means reapplying every few weeks, and most people do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            VCI — vapour corrosion inhibitor — paper is the option most workshops have never tried and should. It releases a vapour that settles on every metal surface in an enclosed space, protecting tools you did not individually treat, including the undersides and inside the sockets. A sheet in each drawer lasts a year or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paste wax deserves a mention for anything you handle often. Oil attracts dust and transfers to your hands; wax dries to a hard film that does neither, which makes it the right choice for plane soles, table saw tops and the outside of tools you grip.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Storage That Helps
          </h2>

          <p>
            How tools are kept matters nearly as much as what they are treated with.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep steel off concrete.</strong> Concrete floors wick moisture from the ground continuously and stay cold, so a toolbox on the slab is sitting in the dampest, coldest air in the room. A pallet, a shelf or castors solve it for nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dry tools before they go away.</strong> Not just visibly dry — a tool that has been used outdoors in cold weather will be below the dew point when it comes in, and will collect condensation in the box. Let it warm up in the open before closing the drawer on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Avoid sealed plastic without desiccant.</strong> A watertight case traps whatever humidity was in the air when you closed it, and then cycles it against the tools with every temperature swing. Sealed is only better than open if there is desiccant inside.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not store tools in foam without checking it.</strong> Closed-cell foam inserts are fine, but open-cell foam and some fabric liners absorb and hold moisture against the steel. If a tool comes out of its cutout with a rust outline the exact shape of the foam, that is the cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Handle with clean hands where you can.</strong> Skin acids and salt from sweat leave fingerprints that etch into bare steel within days in a humid space. It is why old tools often show rust in the shape of where they were gripped.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="removing-rust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Removing Rust That Is Already There
          </h2>

          <p>
            Existing rust must come off before any protection goes on, because it holds moisture and will continue corroding underneath a coating. Work from the gentlest method upward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Light Surface Rust</h3>

          <p>
            Oil and a grey abrasive pad, or fine steel wool. This removes the oxide without cutting into the underlying steel, and for the light orange bloom on a socket it takes a minute per tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Moderate Rust</h3>

          <p>
            A soak in white vinegar for a few hours dissolves iron oxide effectively and cheaply. Watch it — leave tools in for a day and the acid begins attacking the good steel and leaves the surface grey and etched. Rinse thoroughly, dry immediately and completely, then oil, because freshly stripped steel flash-rusts within hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heavy or Pitted Rust</h3>

          <p>
            Commercial rust removers based on oxalic or phosphoric acid, or electrolysis using a battery charger and washing soda solution. Electrolysis is slow, remarkably effective, and does not remove sound metal — which makes it the right method for tools worth saving.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Not to Do</h3>

          <p>
            Aggressive wire wheels and coarse abrasives remove rust and a layer of tool with it, which ruins the fit of sockets and the edges of anything sharp. And pitting is permanent — once corrosion has eaten into the surface, cleaning reveals the craters rather than removing them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-rust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Protecting a Tool You Have Just Cleaned
          </h2>

          <p>
            Freshly derusted steel is more vulnerable than it was before, because whatever passive layer or plating it had is now gone. The window is hours, not days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dry it completely and immediately, with heat if you can — a hairdryer, a warm oven at low temperature, or simply a warm room with moving air. Water trapped in pits is what causes the tool to bloom orange again overnight after a vinegar bath.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then apply protection while it is still warm, because warm metal draws oil into the surface as it cools. Light machine oil is fine; a corrosion-inhibiting spray is better for tools going into storage rather than back into use.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tools that were badly pitted, accept that they will rust again sooner than undamaged ones — the pits hold moisture and there is more surface area exposed. These are the ones to keep oiled and to check occasionally, rather than the ones to trust in a damp corner.
          </p>

          <p>
            And fix the conditions before putting them back. Cleaning a set of rusty sockets and returning them to the same damp garage means doing the same work again next year, which is the most common outcome of a weekend spent derusting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-rusts-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Rusts First, and Why
          </h2>

          <p>
            Corrosion is not uniform across a toolbox, and the pattern tells you what to protect.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Impact sockets and black-finished tools</strong> go first. Black oxide and phosphate finishes offer far less protection than chrome plating — they are thin conversion coatings intended to flex, not to seal. Anything black in your set needs oiling that the chrome does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bare machined surfaces</strong> are next: plane soles, chisel backs, vice jaws, saw plates, the unplated interior of some sockets. Anywhere the steel was ground or machined and never coated is exposed by design.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cut and chipped areas</strong> on plated tools. A chrome socket with a chip in the plating rusts at that point and then under the surrounding chrome, lifting it. This is why a scratched plated tool deteriorates faster than an honestly bare one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Springs and mechanisms</strong> inside ratchets and pliers, which nobody inspects. Rust in a ratchet head is the failure people discover when the tool starts skipping, long after it could have been prevented with a drop of oil at the pivot once a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chrome-plated hand sockets, by contrast, are remarkably tolerant of poor conditions and will generally survive years of mild neglect without complaint. If your time is limited, spend it on the black tools, the bare surfaces and the moving parts — and let the shiny ones look after themselves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Rust as a Water Problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is a humidity problem. Tools that never get wet still rust if the air around them sits above 60 percent relative humidity through the seasons. A dehumidifier protects every tool in the room, including the ones you never get round to oiling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on Water-Displacing Spray</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Products like WD-40 are excellent for driving moisture out of a wet mechanism and poor as long-term protection — the film is thin and evaporates within weeks. For storage, use a proper corrosion inhibitor, paste wax or VCI paper.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing a Toolbox on a Concrete Floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete wicks ground moisture continuously and stays cold, so the box sits in the dampest, coldest air in the room and collects condensation inside. A shelf, a pallet or castors fixes it for nothing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Tools in Vinegar Overnight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A few hours removes rust; a full day starts dissolving the good steel and leaves a grey etched surface. And freshly stripped steel flash-rusts within hours, so it has to be dried completely and oiled straight away.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning Tools and Returning Them to the Same Conditions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most common wasted weekend in a workshop. If the humidity that caused the rust is unchanged, the tools bloom again within a season. Fix the air first, then clean the tools.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my tools rusting in a garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Control the humidity first — a dehumidifier holding the space below 60 percent protects everything in it. Then add barriers: VCI paper in closed drawers, a wipe of light oil or paste wax on bare steel, and keep the toolbox off the concrete floor, which stays cold and wicks moisture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity level causes tools to rust?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Corrosion becomes practically significant above about 60 percent relative humidity and proceeds quickly above 70. Below 50 percent it is slow enough to ignore on coated tools. Temperature swings matter as much as the average, because condensation forms whenever steel is colder than the air around it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is WD-40 good for preventing rust on tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is good at displacing water after a wet job and poor as long-term protection, because the film it leaves is thin and evaporates within weeks. Use it to dry out a wet mechanism, then follow with light machine oil, paste wax or a dedicated corrosion inhibitor for actual storage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is VCI paper and does it work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vapour corrosion inhibitor paper releases a vapour that settles on every metal surface in an enclosed space, so it protects tools you never individually treated — including socket interiors and undersides. A sheet in each closed drawer lasts one to two years and is one of the cheapest protections available.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I remove rust from hand tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Work from gentlest upward: oil and a grey abrasive pad for light surface rust, a few hours in white vinegar for moderate rust, and electrolysis or a phosphoric acid remover for heavy pitting. Avoid coarse wire wheels, which remove sound metal and ruin socket fit and cutting edges.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will rusted tools still work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Light surface rust is cosmetic and cleans off. Pitting is permanent and matters where precision does — a pitted socket grips less well, a pitted chisel back will not polish flat. Rust inside a ratchet mechanism is the serious case, because it causes skipping and is rarely noticed until it fails.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do my black impact sockets rust but the chrome ones do not?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Black oxide and phosphate finishes are thin conversion coatings designed to flex with the steel under impact, not to seal it, so they offer far less corrosion protection than chrome plating. Anything black in your set needs a wipe of oil that the chrome sockets genuinely do not.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

